---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Graves from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Graves.astro";
import g from "../data/graves.json";
const shared = g.rows.filter((r) => r.shared);
const many = g.rows.filter((r) => r.n > 1 && !r.shared);
const single = g.rows.filter((r) => r.n === 1);

/* The two tables - those holding more than one and those holding one - become
   one list with a filter, because they were the same table split by a number.
   The country comes off the end of the place string, which is how this archive
   has always written them: «Chatham, UK». */
const country = (place) => String(place).split(",").pop().trim();
const graves = g.rows.map((r) => ({
  place: r.place,
  country: country(r.place),
  shared: !!r.shared || r.n > 1,
  people: r.people.map((p) => ({
    name: p.name, href: `/people/${p.slug}`, life: p.life || p.died || "",
  })),
}));
---
<Base title="Graves and Burials" eyebrow={`${g.buried} burials · ${g.rows.length} places`}
  dek="Where this family is buried, gathered from the tree — including two plot references that two different people are both supposed to occupy.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Of the people this archive publishes, <strong>{g.buried}</strong> have a recorded
    burial place across <strong>{g.rows.length}</strong> cemeteries and gardens. Almost all of it
    comes from the family tree and almost none of it has been checked against a cemetery register.
    <span class="chip inf">from the tree, unverified</span></p>
  </section>

  <Blk label="The thing worth looking at" title="Two plots, four people">
    <p>A cemetery plot reference is precise: portion, section, grave number. When the same reference
    appears against two people it is either <strong>quietly true</strong> — families did share
    graves, and routinely — or it is a <strong>copy-and-paste error</strong> propagated through an
    online tree. <strong>This archive cannot tell which, and says so.</strong></p>

    <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.2rem">
      {shared.map((r) => (
        <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--ochre);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
          <div><strong>{r.place}</strong></div>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.4rem">
            {r.people.map((p, i) => (
              <>{i > 0 && " · "}<a href={u(`/people/${p.slug}`)}>{p.name}</a>
              {p.life && <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"> ({p.life})</span>}</>
            ))}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <Note>
      <strong>The two read very differently.</strong> At Toowong, a father and the son who died
      thirteen years after him — entirely ordinary, and probably true. At Lutwyche, a woman and her
      son's wife, who died five years before her. Also possible, and much more the sort of thing a
      records-matcher invents. <strong>One email to the Brisbane City Council cemetery register
      settles both in a minute</strong>, and it is now on the list.
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>The queue →</a> · <a href={u("/the-women/")}>The Lutwyche question →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <Graves rows={graves} u={u} />
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Arthur Hartley Sneyd is not on this page.</strong>
    He has no known grave — the army's own form recorded his burial place as "Not yet to hand", and it
    never came. <a href={u("/finding-arthur/")}>The search for him →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
